    <style name="Normal Text" builtin="1" builtin_index="0" font="Courier Prime" size="12"/>
    <style name="Scene Heading" builtin="1" builtin_index="1" basestylename="Normal Text" style_enter="Action" style_tab_after="Action" font="Courier Prime" size="12" spacebefore="2.0" keepwithnext="1" allcaps="1"/>
    <style name="Action" builtin="1" builtin_index="2" basestylename="Normal Text" style_tab_before="Scene Heading" style_tab_after="Character" font="Courier Prime" size="12" spacebefore="1.0"/>
    <style name="Character" builtin="1" builtin_index="3" basestylename="Normal Text" style_enter="Dialogue" style_tab_before="Action" style_tab_after="Transition" font="Courier Prime" size="12" spacebefore="1.0" keepwithnext="1" leftindent="508" rightindent="64" allcaps="1"/>
    <style name="Parenthetical" builtin="1" builtin_index="4" basestylename="Normal Text" style_enter="Dialogue" style_tab_before="Dialogue" style_tab_after="Dialogue" font="Courier Prime" size="12" spacebefore="0.0" keepwithnext="1" leftindent="381" firstlineindent="-25" rightindent="508"/>
    <style name="Dialogue" builtin="1" builtin_index="5" basestylename="Normal Text" style_enter="Action" style_tab_before="Parenthetical" style_tab_after="Parenthetical" font="Courier Prime" size="12" spacebefore="0.0" leftindent="254" rightindent="381"/>
    <style name="Transition" builtin="1" builtin_index="6" basestylename="Normal Text" style_enter="Scene Heading" style_tab_after="Action" font="Courier Prime" size="12" spacebefore="1.0" align="right" leftindent="1016" rightindent="102" allcaps="1"/>
    <style name="Shot" builtin="1" builtin_index="7" basestylename="Normal Text" style_enter="Action" style_tab_after="Action" font="Courier Prime" size="12" spacebefore="2.0" keepwithnext="1" allcaps="1"/>
    <style name="Lyrics" basestylename="Dialogue" italic="1"/>
  </styles>
  <paragraphs>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <method_storyteller kind="section" level="1"/>
      <text>ACT ONE</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <method_storyteller kind="synopsis"/>
      <text>Two people walk up to a station that has stopped talking.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="1">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Wind comes down off the ridge in long flat gusts, pushing the grass one way and then, for no reason, the other.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A road climbs the ridge in switchbacks. Nobody has driven it since the washout, and the grass is taking it back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA, 40s, walks it with a pack on and four days in her legs. She has the face of someone who fixes things and is never thanked.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX, 50s, walks behind her with a paper map folded to the right square. He does not look at it. He looks at the road, and counts.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-one.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They take the bend. Gravel lets go under Mara&apos;s boot and goes quietly over the edge.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You don&apos;t have to do that out loud.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The map says forty. I&apos;d like to know when it&apos;s lying.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Cloud sits on the ridge above them like a lid somebody forgot to close.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How long has he been up there?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Three weeks. On his own.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They said two.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They say a lot of things.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The road ends at the washout, a bite taken out of the mountain. Mara stops where the road stops, then goes round it on the rock.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-eight.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="2">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA and DELACROIX come up the last of the slope, packs on, bent into the wind. Below them the road they walked is a thin grey line, going all the way down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The station: a prefab hut bolted to the rock, a radio mast guyed four ways, a fuel drum, a generator crouched under a tarp. Everything that can rattle, rattles.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The hut door opens before they reach it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO, 20s, comes out in two jumpers and no coat. Thin. He has the look of someone who has rehearsed what to say to people and forgotten all of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You came.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>We said we would.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>People say that on the radio. It&apos;s different, seeing it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He shakes Delacroix&apos;s hand, then Mara&apos;s, then Delacroix&apos;s again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;re Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;m Teo. Sorry. Come in, it&apos;s warmer. It isn&apos;t, but it&apos;s out of the wind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The wind leans on all three of them. The mast answers it with a low note, like someone humming through their teeth.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s the mast. You get used to it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Do you?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>No.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo walks them round the station at a pace that makes it hard to see any of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Fuel. Water. Rain gauge. It&apos;s been fine the whole time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He points up at the mast without looking at it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It sings in a north wind. One long note. In a west wind it&apos;s two, and they don&apos;t agree.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And an east wind?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>We don&apos;t get an east wind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He thinks about it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>We got one once.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Mara crouches by the generator and lifts the tarp. Oil on the housing, old and new. One bolt is missing. Another turns in her fingers with no thread left to hold it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She looks at it for a long moment. Then she lets the tarp fall.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Well?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It runs.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>That is all she says. Delacroix has worked with her long enough to count what she leaves out.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I kept it running. Every day. I wrote it down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I believe you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo nods too many times and goes inside. The wind shuts the door behind him, hard.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="3">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One room. A cot, a table, one chair. A tarp lashed over a gap in the north wall where a panel used to be. It breathes with the weather.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the wall above the radio, a map of the valley. Four pins in it, running down the line to the lowest station.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(into the handset)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base, this is Field Station. We&apos;re up.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Static. Then a voice, patient, a long way off.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Field Station, Iversen. Good to hear you. How&apos;s the boy?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix glances at Teo, who is shelving their tins in order of size.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Tidy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Pickup is tomorrow, first light. Relief team brings the new keeper up and the three of you down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>First light.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Weather permitting. It always permits, until it doesn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He sets the handset down. Mara is looking at the map.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Why four pins?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One for every station on the line. We&apos;re the top one.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He touches the lowest pin, then takes his finger away as if it were warm.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="4">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RADIO MAST - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The light goes early up here. What is left of it is the colour of weak tea.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA climbs the mast, harness clipped to the ladder, a spanner through her belt. The wind pulls at her like it wants her for something.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX stands at the foot, the safety line paid out through his gloves.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How many rungs?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I don&apos;t count them.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Forty-four. I counted for you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She reaches the antenna. It hangs off true, its bracket worked loose by a winter of north wind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Can I ask you something?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;re going to.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Why did you take this job? You could fix things anywhere. Somewhere with walls.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A gust takes the mast and leans it an inch. She waits it out, cheek against the cold steel, eyes shut.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Below her, the line goes taut in his hands and stays taut until the gust is gone.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nothing up here pretends to work.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix thinks about that. Decides not to agree with it out loud.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She sets the spanner to the bracket. Two turns. The antenna comes true, and the mast&apos;s low note changes pitch, very slightly, as if it has noticed.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Is that it?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She doesn&apos;t come down. She is looking past him, past the hut, out across the valley to the far ridge.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A light. One small steady point of it, low on the far slope, where the map shows nothing at all.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It does not flicker. Nothing out there has any reason to be lit.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Mara? You&apos;re losing the light.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The point goes out, like somebody closing a hand around it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Coming down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She unclips and starts down, rung by rung. She does not mention it. She will not mention it for some time.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="5">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - EVENING</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Three of them round a table built for one. Tins on a camping stove. The tarp on the north wall breathes in, out.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO eats fast, like the food might be taken back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Four pins. Tell me about them.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>We call down the line at six every evening. Everybody answers. We all say goodnight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Everybody?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo puts his fork down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The fourth hasn&apos;t answered. Nine days.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A pause. The stove ticks as it cools.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nine days, and you didn&apos;t report it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I did. Every night, after I called them, I reported it to base.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody answered that either.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix looks at Mara. Mara looks at the radio. The radio hisses and says nothing, the way it has said nothing for nine days.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Iversen answered today.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Iversen answered you.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="6">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Wind, and under the wind a cold that means business.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX walks the ridge with a torch, the beam no use against anything further off than his own boots.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He finds the marker cairn where the map says it will be: a chest-high stack of flat stones, sighted down the valley toward the fourth station. In clear weather you could see its lamp from here.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>There is no lamp tonight. There is no clear weather.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He sets the torch on the ground and looks at the cairn properly.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It has been rebuilt. Carefully. Every stone seated, every gap chinked with smaller stones. Nobody did this in a hurry, and nobody did it for the weather.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Hello?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The wind takes the word and does not bring it back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He knows this cairn. He built the last one himself, eleven years ago, and he knows how many stones went into it. Twenty-six.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He counts them. His lips move.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-five.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He counts again, slower, touching each one with a gloved finger, top to bottom.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-five.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One short. Whoever rebuilt it built it well and built it one stone short, and a careful person does not make that mistake by accident.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He sweeps the torch around his feet. Grass. Rock. No stone lying loose.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Down in the valley, where the fourth station should be, there is only dark.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He switches the torch off and stands in it for a moment, to see what his eyes will do.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Nothing out there changes. Nothing out there has changed for nine days.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He turns the torch back on and walks back toward the hut, faster than he came.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="7">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - LATER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Mara is asleep on the cot in her boots. Teo is not asleep, and not pretending to be.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The radio crackles. Delacroix comes in with the cold still on him and goes straight to the handset.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Field Station, Iversen. Field Station, do you read?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Go ahead.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Weather&apos;s closing over the pass. Relief is delayed.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Delayed how long?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Static, for long enough that Teo sits up.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-three hours, earliest. We&apos;ll hold here until it...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The hiss closes over the rest.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Iversen. Come back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Nothing. Delacroix sets the handset down gently, as if it might still be listening.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He takes a pencil from his pocket and writes 23 on the wall beside the map, small and neat. He looks at it. Underlines it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Was that him? Was that really him?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Go to sleep, Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="8">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The wind has found a new direction and does not like that one either.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO wheels the quad bike out of the lean-to. Headlamp on. A jerrycan strapped across the back, and a second map, the survey copy, rolled under his arm.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA comes out after him, pulling on her coat.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It&apos;s four hours down if the track&apos;s clear. I&apos;ll see them. I&apos;ll come back and tell you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Don&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nine days. Somebody has to go and look.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>In the morning. With us.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>In the morning there&apos;ll be a reason to wait until the afternoon.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He is not wrong, and she knows it, and that is the worst part.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Call us when you get there.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I always call.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He kicks the bike over. It catches first time, which feels like a verdict.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX is in the doorway now, the lamp behind him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Four hours down. Four back. The relief is twenty-three.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then I&apos;ll be back before them.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo pulls out onto the track. The headlamp swings across the rock, across the fuel drum, and settles on the way down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They watch the tail light go. Red, small, smaller. It dips below the lip of the ridge, comes up once more on the next switchback, and then does not come up again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Mara keeps looking at the place where it was.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You could have stopped him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I could have slowed him down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Behind them, under its tarp, the generator coughs.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Once. A dry, small sound, like someone clearing their throat before bad news. Then it runs on, even and ordinary, as if nothing happened.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Neither of them says anything. Delacroix looks at his watch and does not say the time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Transition"/>
      <text>CUT TO:</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="9">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. LOWER STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A hut like theirs, lower and older, in a fold of the valley where the wind cannot quite reach and so sulks instead.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A headlamp comes down the track and stops. TEO kills the engine. The silence is enormous.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The door of the hut is open. Not blown open. Propped, with a stone.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Hello? It&apos;s Teo. From the top.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Nothing. He goes in.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Inside, the station is neat. The cot is made. A mug stands on the table with a skin on its cold tea.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The radio is on. Its dial glows a soft green, the only light in the room.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo leans in to read the frequency. He knows the number. It is theirs.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Beside the radio a notebook lies open. Line after line of the same entry, in a hand that gets smaller down the page. Six o&apos;clock. Six o&apos;clock. Six o&apos;clock.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Someone has been listening. Every night. And never once said a word.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo picks up the handset. His thumb finds the key.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He holds it down.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="10">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The generator coughs once and dies. MARA works by the light of her phone, hands steady, and does not look up.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Give it a minute. It always comes back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And if it doesn&apos;t?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She finds the housing bolt by feel. Turns it a quarter turn.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then we do this in the dark.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The wind takes the tarp on the north wall and holds it open like a door nobody opened.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s not an answer.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It&apos;s the one I have.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He crosses to the window. Outside, the ridge is a black line against a slightly less black sky.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nineteen hours until the relief team. I counted.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then stop counting.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The generator catches. Dies again. Catches.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Light comes up amber and unsteady, and the room assembles itself around them: the cot, the radio, the map with its four pins.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>There. Told you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You told me a minute. That was six.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I rounded.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She wipes her hands on her thigh and sits, finally, like the sitting costs her something.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You should sleep.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You should stop saying that.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A long moment. The radio hisses, says nothing, hisses.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>If the relief doesn&apos;t come.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They&apos;ll come.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>If.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then we walk out the way we walked in, and it takes four days instead of one, and you complain the entire time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I don&apos;t complain.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;re complaining now.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He almost laughs. It is the first honest sound either of them has made all night.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Get some sleep. I&apos;ll take the radio.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She doesn&apos;t argue, which frightens him more than the dark did.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Transition"/>
      <text>CUT TO:</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="11">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - DAWN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Grey light. The tarp is down. The map is gone from the wall.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX sits exactly where he sat, the handset still in his fist, asleep with his eyes open the way soldiers learn.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The radio speaks. One word, clipped, in a voice neither of them has heard.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He is awake before he knows he is awake.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Say again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Nothing. The hiss closes over the word like water.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Say again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He turns the dial one notch, then back, then one notch the other way, hunting the frequency the way you hunt a name you have almost remembered.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (O.S.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>What was it?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She is in the doorway. She has not slept and does not pretend otherwise.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>A voice. One word. I couldn&apos;t hold it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Which word?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He looks at the handset, then at her.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ours.</text>
    </para>
    <para synopsis="The station listens, and Teo goes looking.">
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Outside, very far off, something that is not the wind moves along the ridge.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="12">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Frost on everything that held still overnight. The fuel drum wears it. The mast does not.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA crouches at the mouth of the lean-to. Two lines in the white, a tyre&apos;s width apart, run out of it and over the lip of the ridge. Nothing has come back over them.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX comes out with two mugs and gives her one. She holds it for the heat.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Anything?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He went down. He didn&apos;t come up. Frost came at about two. Anything coming back after that would have broken it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She stands. Her knees have an opinion about it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nothing broke it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix looks at his watch. Eleven times since dawn. He knows it was eleven.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Four hours down. Four back. He&apos;d have been here by six. It&apos;s ten past nine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>So he stayed. Or he&apos;s asleep in a warm hut with his boots off. Which is the one you&apos;re not saying.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;m saying it now. And the relief is thirteen hours. If they keep to it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They won&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She tips her tea out on the frost. It steams, and then it doesn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="13">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A long low room at the bottom of the valley, where weather happens to other people. A wall map, the four stations in red tape. A chalked weather board: PASS, RIDGE, LAKE. A radio set the size of a wardrobe, hissing. BASE STAFF come and go with mugs. Nobody hurries.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>IVERSEN at the set, 60s, older than his voice. A cardigan buttoned one button wrong. Beside him the DISPATCHER, 30s, sleeves pushed up, her big ruled log open.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(into the handset)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Pass relay, base. Give me a carrier.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A tone, thin, like a kettle in somebody else&apos;s house. Three seconds. Gone.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Four times since seven.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It&apos;s the cold. It&apos;s only lately it minds.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She writes: RELAY. CARRIER 3 SEC. DROPPED.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen turns back through the green book the night staff keep, looking for the boy&apos;s last report. He stops. Nine nights, nine hands, the same words.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>FOURTH STATION NO ANSWER AT ROLL CALL. NOTED.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Who passed it on?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody. It says noted.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He runs a finger down the initials. The seventh night&apos;s are his own. He looks at them like a photograph of himself he doesn&apos;t remember being taken.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I remember a boy reading out a list. I thought he was being thorough.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He closes the book and lays his hand flat on it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Write it in yours. Fourth station, no contact, nine days. Reported nightly by the top station. Not acted on.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>By who?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>By base.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She writes it, and does not soften it. He nods once.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="14">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - LAB - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A lean-to off the hut, its roof sheet ticking in the sun. A bench, spare parts in labelled tins, logbooks numbered in white paint.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA takes down the newest and sits on the only stool.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo&apos;s hand: upright, schoolroom. One page a day, ruled into six boxes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DAY 1. WIND N, ONE NOTE. GEN 6 HRS. RAIN 2 MM. CALLED THE LINE. 4 ANSWERED. REPORTED TO BASE.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Nothing missing, nothing crossed out. A ruler every day for three weeks, for nobody.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Day 13: CALLED THE LINE. 3 ANSWERED. She slows. Day 17, squeezed under the box: REPORTED TO BASE AGAIN. She counts to the end with her thumb. Nine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Day 21 is yesterday. GEN COUGHED ONCE. VISITORS: 2. CALLED THE LINE. 3 ANSWERED.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She reads VISITORS: 2 twice. Something in her face almost goes, and doesn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the shelf, a wooden case lined in green felt, six hollows, five valves. The sixth hollow&apos;s felt has not faded. Not empty long. In the lid, an inventory card with Teo&apos;s ticks, and older brown ink: SEVEN-PIN. NEW PATTERN SETS ONLY.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Pinned above the bench: LINE SETS. TOP AND TWO, NEW PATTERN. THREE AND FOUR, OLD PATTERN.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She looks from the card to the sheet, and closes the lid very gently.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="15">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RADIO MAST - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Clear, for once. The valley lies open below like a hand.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA near the top, a compass on a lanyard. DELACROIX at the foot, the line through his gloves.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Where&apos;s the lower station from here?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One six two degrees. Eleven kilometres, give or take the hill in the way.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>This is aimed at base. Nobody&apos;s had a reason to move it in thirty years.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base is the one that answers.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base is the one that isn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She walks the antenna round against the wind. The mast&apos;s note slides down, unhappily. The line goes taut; he takes the weight without being asked.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Do you want to know how far up you are?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>No.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He doesn&apos;t say it. His lips move anyway.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One six two. She sets the bolts. The note settles lower, with something under it that wasn&apos;t there yesterday.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>If he&apos;s at Four. If their set&apos;s on. If it&apos;s on our frequency.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s a lot of ifs.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It&apos;s three. You were counting.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She comes down. He lets her reach the ground without a number.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="16">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The hiss has a new floor under it, like a road a long way off. DELACROIX at the set. MARA behind him, still in her harness.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(into the handset)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Four, this is the top. Do you read.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A click, and inside it one word, cut at both ends as if with scissors.</text>
    </para>
    <para note="Radio playback on set.">
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text></text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ours.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s the word. From this morning.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(keys)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Say again, over.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He watches his second hand.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(under his breath)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Fifty-eight. Fifty-nine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>CLOSE ON THE RADIO.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The signal needle lifts off its pin. Trembles.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The needle drops. The hiss.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It wasn&apos;t somebody on our frequency this morning. It was him, at Four. Telling us their set&apos;s tuned to ours.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One word, then a minute. A valve that won&apos;t stay seated. It touches long enough for a word, lets go, and has to warm again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Can he fix it?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>There&apos;s a seven-pin valve gone from the lab. Theirs is the old pattern.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Meaning.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Meaning no.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(keys)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Teo, it&apos;s Delacroix. We hear you. Is Ansel with you?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He counts two minutes under his breath and stops at a hundred and twenty, because nothing happens at a hundred and twenty.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He writes on the wall under the 23: 11.40. TEO AT FOUR. He doesn&apos;t underline this one.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="17">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. LOWER STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Daylight makes the lower station smaller. The cot, made. The mug, its tea gone leathery.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO on an upturned crate at the set. He has not slept. On the table, a row of pencil marks, one for every try since dawn.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>At sixty on the station clock, he keys.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ours. It&apos;s Teo, I&apos;m at Four, there&apos;s nobody here, the set&apos;s...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The red lamp on the panel lit on the first word and has already gone out. A dead key has a softer click; he knows it by now.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He adds a pencil mark. Waits for sixty.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The lamp lights on the word and dies on the end of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He takes a coin to the back panel. Inside, a row of warm valves. One leans in its socket like a loose tooth. He pushes it upright; it leans again. Its spring clip is cracked through.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>From his coat he unwraps a twist of cloth: the lab&apos;s valve. Seven pins. Theirs has nine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You were never going to fit.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He pockets it. The set needs pliers, a clip, solder and Mara. He has a coin. He screws the panel back on, because leaving it off would be untidy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The notebook lies square to the table edge. Six o&apos;clock, six o&apos;clock, the hand shrinking. On the last written page, pressed hard enough to dent the paper, an arrow running off the edge, and two letters.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A.V.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo follows the line of the arrow across the table. It points at the door.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="18">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. LOWER STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Birches, a stream out of sight, a rain gauge nobody has emptied.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO sits on the doorstep and picks up what props the door: a flat grey stone the size of a book. On its face, half rubbed away by nine nights of damp, a chalk arrow. As it lay, it pointed down the valley, toward a lake you can&apos;t see from here.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Everyone learns the sign their first week, from a card at base between the knots and the first aid. He has never seen one used.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Gone this way.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It is not a valley stone. Those are round, washed smooth. This one is split flat and blue-grey, a ridge stone. He doesn&apos;t know what that means. Delacroix would. It goes in his pack anyway: somebody left it for somebody, and he is somebody.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Without it the door swings to and latches.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Round the back, the diesel drum is empty; it answers his knuckle like a bell. One jerrycan marked PETROL, half full or less. He fills the quad slowly, not wasting a drop.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Through the window we see him bend over the notebook and write one line under the arrow. Then he rides off down the valley, the way the arrow said.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="19">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Midday. The wind has come round west; the mast sings two notes that don&apos;t agree.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX on the bench, lacing his boots like a man deciding something. MARA in the doorway, arms folded.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The relief gets as far as the washout. Somebody meets them there and takes them on to Four by the old supply path.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And somebody stays with the set.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character" dualdialogue="1"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;re faster than me. You go down, I sit by the radio. Nobody can count a minute like I can.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You know the road and the relief listen to you. You go. If the set breaks I can fix it, and if he keys again I&apos;ll know what&apos;s wrong by the sound.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They stop at the same moment. The mast fills in, not agreeing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One at a time. You first.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Mara doesn&apos;t answer. She goes into the hut. He counts without meaning to. Twelve. Thirteen.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She comes out with his pack and sets it at his feet. Packed. Bivvy bag, water, two tins with the opener taped to one, the map folded to the right square, the spare torch with its batteries turned round so they won&apos;t drain, which is a thing he does, and she has noticed.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She is not going to say another word.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>While I was talking.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He puts it on. She has done the straps to his length.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>If he keys again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;ll write it on your wall. Under the twenty-three.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>At the lip of the ridge he lifts a hand. She lifts one back. Then she goes in and sits in his chair, still warm, and puts on the headphones.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="20">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX coming down alone. Going down this road, the gravel wants to go with you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-eight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The washout. He stops to think, where Mara didn&apos;t. Then he goes round, one hand on the rock, not looking down, the only way he has ever found to do it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-seven.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The turning circle. An oil stain, a marker post with its paint gone. The end of the road for anything with wheels.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Down the switchbacks, as far as the cloud allows: no vehicle, no sound of one. A raven, going somewhere else.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He sits on the marker post. It was not made to be sat on.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirteen hours.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(checks his watch)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He settles in to wait the way he waits for everything: upright, patient, keeping count.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="21">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para note="Second unit: wide only.">
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text></text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The far side of the range, the dry side. A gravel fire road cut in long pale loops through dark heather.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On it, very small, the quad. A thread of dust stands up behind it and leans away on the wind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Far below, through a gap in the hills, the lake: a grey coin with nothing on it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The quad takes a long bend and the hill hides it. The dust hangs on, then the wind takes that too.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="22">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Afternoon light in slabs on the floor. On the weather board, PASS reads CLOSED.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The DISPATCHER on the telephone, writing as she talks. IVERSEN at the set behind her, listening with half of himself.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(into the phone)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The fourth station. Nine days, no contact.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(listens)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Yes, nine. I know. I&apos;ve written it down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen&apos;s shoulders heard that.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The pass is shut and the washout&apos;s gone. It&apos;ll have to be your side, the fire road.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(listens)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Solli. Good. Tell Solli to look for a quad, and a young man who&apos;ll tell her everything twice.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She writes: RANGER STATION. FIRE ROAD AT FIRST LIGHT. SOLLI.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>First light. I asked for sooner.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I know you did.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(keys)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Pass relay, base. Give me a carrier.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The tone comes up, clean, holding. For a second his face is a much younger man&apos;s.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Field Station, Iversen, through the relay. Do you...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A crackle like a match struck. The tone goes, and the hiss that comes back is deader than before.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s eleven.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Write it down.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="23">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - LAB - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The last light lies along the bench like a ruler.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA has the spare transmitter open: old pattern, brown enamel, stencilled FOR PARTS. Its insides lie on a cloth in the order they came out. A jeweller&apos;s loupe in one eye.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She lifts out a nine-pin valve, checks each pin against the lamp, and sets it apart like a guest of honour. Then its socket. The clip is whole, and rusted solid.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>No. You&apos;re no good either. So the valve&apos;s fine. It&apos;s the seat. It leans, it drops out. It wants something to hold it that isn&apos;t a clip. Something thin. Something that springs. You&apos;ve got tins. You&apos;ve got...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She hears herself. The voice in the room is hers, and it has been going for a while.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She stops. The roof sheet ticks as it cools.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She picks up an empty tobacco tin with a hinged lid and turns it under the lamp, looking at how thin the metal is.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="24">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Amber light. The lake nearer now, a sheet of pewter in a bowl of hills.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO on the quad, on a long straight. The engine coughs: once, dry and small, exactly the sound the generator made the night he left.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It runs on fifty yards. Then it coughs twice and stops, and the quad rolls on in silence until the gravel slows it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>No.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The starter catches nothing. He kneels by the tank. The petrol smell is too strong. Under the fuel line his hand comes away wet; a drip forms on the union, falls, forms again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Back up the road, a thin dark line on the gravel, all the way round the bend.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You could have said.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He pushes the quad onto the verge, where a truck could pass, and leaves the key in.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>His headlamp swings across the roadside rock and stops. At the height of a man&apos;s hand, a chalk arrow, pointing on down the road.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He puts his finger near it, not on it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Hello.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The last of the light goes off the lake.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="25">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A knuckle of rock on a side spur above the washout. The far slope lies open: Mara&apos;s view from the mast, two nights ago, at this hour.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX comes up onto it breathing hard. He is here because of something he saw that night, which was not a light. It was Mara&apos;s face at the top of the mast, looking past him at the far slope, very still. Then not mentioning it. He counts what she leaves out.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He finds her line on the map and sights his pencil along it: a low shoulder where the map shows nothing at all.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The light goes from green to grey to black. Nothing on the shoulder. Not a flicker. He waits a while longer, because being sure is not the same as knowing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>So somebody lit it. And it isn&apos;t there tonight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He puts a small pencil cross on the shoulder.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You moved.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It frightens him a little, how much better that is than the alternative.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="26">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - BUNKHOUSE - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A second small hut, unheated, unused this season. Cold blankets and mice.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA comes in with a torch. Four bunks. A shelf of grey blankets, each with a stencilled number. She takes two, then a third, because the night will be colder than this.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The beam goes up the wall above the bunks, and stops.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Names, carved into the boards, crowding each other. Initials, dates, a bad drawing of the mast, singing. Relief teams, year after year, on the one wall nobody would paint.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>ANSEL, in block capitals, a date that makes him a young man. Further along, shallower: ANSEL, fifteen years later. And low on a bottom-bunk post, nearly at the floor, as if done lying down: ANSEL. No date. The wood in the letters is still pale.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Pinned above it, a photograph: four keepers in this yard on a summer day, a kettle on the step, the mast behind them straight. All four smiling. Nobody up here smiles like that now. On the back, in pencil: HANDOVER. ALL FOUR.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The man on the left is Ansel. She knows it before she knows how: the same block capitals in the way he stands.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She pins it back through the same hole and goes out. The beam leaves the names one by one, and they go back to being a wall.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="27">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Six o&apos;clock, full dark, the generator off to save fuel.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA on the footing of the mast by the field jack, a weatherproof box bolted to its leg. The handheld plugged in. Teo&apos;s logbook on her knee, a clean page ruled by headlamp into six boxes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Roll call. This is the top station calling the line. Two, this is the top.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A voice comes back, small, far off and cheerful about it: a man she has never met, answering his station&apos;s name. The words are lost. The shape of them is right.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thank you, Two. Three, this is the top.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She gives it a full minute, because Delacroix would. Nothing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Four, this is the top.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She waits, knowing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Top station. Here. Goodnight, all.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She writes in Teo&apos;s boxes, as near to his hand as she can make her own.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DAY 22. WIND W, TWO NOTES. GEN COUGHED TWICE. VISITORS: 1. CALLED THE LINE. 2 ANSWERED. REPORTED TO BASE.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She hasn&apos;t, yet. She keys.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base, top station. Two answered. Three no answer tonight. Four no answer, ten days.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Hiss. It is true now, so she leaves it written.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="28">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Dark the way it is only dark a long way from anything.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO walks the middle of the road in the cone of his headlamp. The stone in his pack knocks his spine at every step.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A bend. On a boulder, a chalk arrow.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>There you are.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The next bend. An arrow, lower down the rock.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And you. You&apos;re getting lower. Did you notice?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He holds his hand at his shoulder. Then at his knee. He doesn&apos;t like what that means, so he talks about something else.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I didn&apos;t call the line tonight. First time in twenty-one days. Twenty-two. Mara will have done it. In my boxes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The next arrow is at the foot of a marker post, so low he nearly misses it. Beside it on the gravel, a smear, as if something heavy had been dragged.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s all right. I&apos;ve got it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He waits, the way you wait on the radio after you have said your piece. Nothing answers.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He finds he doesn&apos;t mind. The arrows say the same thing every time: this way. It is the most anyone has said to him in nine days.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="29">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Half the strip lights off. A sandwich at Iversen&apos;s elbow, its corners curling. The DISPATCHER&apos;s coat on her chair; she was meant to go home at four. The wall clock says two minutes past six.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The relay tone comes up under the hiss, clean, holding. IVERSEN sits up.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Faint, in pieces: a woman&apos;s voice calling the line. A station number. A wait the length of a minute. Top station. Here. Goodnight, all.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That isn&apos;t the boy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s the engineer. She&apos;s doing his roll call. In his order.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Her voice again, flatter: two answered, three no answer, four no answer, ten days. A crackle. The tone goes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ninety seconds. About.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Write it down. And write down that the top station is still calling. Write down who&apos;s calling. Write down who isn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Three didn&apos;t answer tonight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then write that.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Her pen is loud in the half-lit room. He keys into the dead hiss, knowing nobody can hear.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Field Station, Iversen. Roll call received.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="30">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE ROAD - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The turning circle at the washout. Cold up off the gravel and down off the sky at once.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX in a bivvy bag against the marker post. No relief. No headlights on the switchbacks, which he could see all the way down now the cloud has lifted.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He looks at his watch, and makes himself put it away. Above him, more stars than anyone needs.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One. Two. Three.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He reads across the sky like a page. He loses his place over the ridge line and goes back to the last one he was sure of.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Forty-one.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It is no good. Every star is a minute, every ten a bend of the road. The hours add themselves up underneath whether he counts them or not.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ninety-nine. A hundred.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>There are plainly a great many more. He gives up on them with something like respect, buttons his watch into the top pocket of the pack, and lies back under the sky, uncounted.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="31">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One candle in a jar. The map back on the wall, four pins catching the light. Beside it, under the 23 and the 11.40. TEO AT FOUR, a new line in a squarer hand: 18.00. ROLL CALL. 2.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The tarp on the north wall breathes in. Out.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA in the chair at the radio, three numbered blankets round her, the handset on her knee, its cord looped once round her wrist so it can&apos;t fall.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The radio hisses. She listens to it the way you listen to someone breathing in the next room.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Her eyes close. She opens them. Outside, the mast holds one long note, and then two.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Her eyes close, and stay closed. Her thumb rests beside the key, not on it. It is the first time she has slept since they came up the road.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The candle burns down a finger&apos;s width. Nothing on the radio all night. It doesn&apos;t stop listening, and neither, quite, does she.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <method_storyteller kind="section" level="1"/>
      <text>ACT TWO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <method_storyteller kind="synopsis"/>
      <text>The lake, the keeper, and a radio that answers.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="32">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. KETTLE LAKE - DAWN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The lake lies in the bottom of the valley like something set down and forgotten. Flat, grey, no wind on it at all. After three days of wind the stillness feels like a held breath.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO comes out of the trees on the last of the arrows, headlamp still on, wet to the shin. His pack sits low on one shoulder, heavier than it should be.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Across a bay, a timber boathouse stands on legs over the water. Smoke from its stove pipe goes straight up, the way smoke never does at the station.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Hello. Over there.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The sound goes out across the water and does not come back. The lake takes it the way the ridge took Delacroix&apos;s, only more politely.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He swings the pack round and checks something inside without taking it out. A flat stone, chalk on one face. He buckles the pack again, both buckles.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(to himself)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Round, then.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He walks the shore: shingle, reeds, a fallen tree he climbs over, and climbs back over, because the far side of it is deeper.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="33">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. BOATHOUSE - DAWN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Planks gone silver, a jetty, a rowboat alongside. A white skiff keel-up on trestles. Nets on a line, stiff with cold.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The door opens. AUD, 70s, steps onto the jetty with an oar held across her body. Not like a paddle. Like the other thing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s far enough.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;m Teo. From the top station. Cold Spring.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Never heard of you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You might have. On the radio. At six.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Say it, then. What you say at six.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Cold Spring calling the line. Cold Spring calling the line. Goodnight, all stations.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The oar comes down an inch.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You say it too fast. And you say goodnight before anybody&apos;s answered.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s so I don&apos;t forget.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The oar comes down the rest of the way. She leans it by the door, within reach.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Aud. You walked.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The bike gave up. Then I followed the arrows. Chalk, one on every bend.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Something moves in her face. She does not let it get far.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Someone came this way. A week ago.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Crawling, mostly.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Is he alive.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Tea first. You&apos;re blue. Then you can see for yourself, and I don&apos;t have to say it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She goes in and leaves the oar where it is. He notices that, and follows.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="34">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BOATHOUSE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One room over the water. Light comes up through the floorboards, moving, so the room seems slightly afloat. A stove red at the seams. A shelf of tins in no order at all, and among them a small radio, receiver only, its dial the same soft green as the one at the lower station.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the cot: ANSEL, 50s, stubble gone most of the way to a beard. Eyes shut. His left leg is splinted with a broken oar handle, bound in net cord in neat, ugly knots.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO holds a mug of tea he has forgotten about. AUD feeds the stove and does not watch him. That is a kindness, and she knows it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Broken below the knee. Fever, on and off. I set it as well as I could, which was not very.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo kneels by the cot.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ansel.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The eyes open and find him. A breath comes out with the shape of a word in it, and no word.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He can&apos;t get it above a whisper. Don&apos;t make him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo takes his hand. Ansel presses it. Once. Twice. Three times. Then lets go, spent.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He did that to me the first night. I think it means he heard you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo sets the tea down on the floor.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I called you. Every night at six, the whole line, and your name last, because you&apos;re the bottom one and you always answer last. I&apos;d count thirty, because that&apos;s how long the relay takes when it&apos;s tired, and then thirty again in case it was more tired. Then I&apos;d say goodnight to you anyway. I thought if I stopped saying it, that would be the night it was true.

And I wrote it down. Called the line, three answered. It&apos;s a good format, it&apos;s the one they taught us, and it hasn&apos;t got anywhere to put the one who didn&apos;t. I kept wanting to write you in the margin. I didn&apos;t, because it&apos;s a logbook. I should have written you in the margin.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I heard you as well.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She points the poker at the shelf.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That set listens. It doesn&apos;t talk. My husband bought it for the weather and was too mean to buy the other half. Nine nights: you, the two in the middle, then you saying goodnight to nothing. He lay there and heard every word.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Has it got a key? Even a click.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It&apos;s got on and it&apos;s got off. We used to look at each other.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo takes the flat stone from his pack and holds it where Ansel can see, chalk arrow up. Ansel&apos;s eyes stay on it. Something in his face lets go.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>His. It was on his doorstep. It means gone this way.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Aud looks at the boy who has carried a stone down a mountain for no reason a sensible person could give. He puts it in his coat pocket, where he can feel it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Drink your tea.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It has gone cold. He drinks all of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="35">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>First light under the trees. A green park truck comes down the gravel switchbacks in low gear. SOLLI, 30s, drives, a thermos wedged between the seats, like the road owes her nothing and she owes it less.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Before the bend: a quad bike nosed into the ditch. Pushed there, not crashed.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She gets out. The quad is cold. The jerrycan on the back rings, empty. Underneath, the fuel line has split along its seam, and a thin dark stripe runs back up the gravel the way the bike came.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(into the handset)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Dispatch, Solli. I&apos;ve got the quad, about the sixth bend down. Fuel line&apos;s split. No rider.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He pushed it off the road. Most people leave them where they die.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She ties a strip of orange tape round the handlebar, so the next person will know someone has already been sorry about it.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="36">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Strip lights, one flickering in a rhythm nobody can fix. The big set, the weather board, a wall map with four red pins down the mountain. BASE STAFF (bg).</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The DISPATCHER, 30s, at her desk, log open. IVERSEN stands behind her, which she has asked him twice not to do.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>...sixth from the top. Maybe seventh. Somebody&apos;s moved the marker posts.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody&apos;s moved the marker posts.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then they&apos;ve moved themselves.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She puts a yellow pin in the fire road, six bends down. She and Iversen look at the distance between it and the lowest red pin.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s walking to the lake.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>There&apos;s nothing at the lake but a woman who hasn&apos;t spoken to anyone since her husband died.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then she&apos;ll be glad of the company.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(into the phone)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base dispatch. Search plane on standby for first light tomorrow, Kettle Lake side. I know about the weather. That&apos;s why I&apos;m asking today.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(radio)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Solli. There&apos;s a man at the washout on the ridge road. Our inspector. Can you fetch him?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Is he hurt?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s counting things. Iversen says that&apos;s normal.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen is already at the big set.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Field Station, Iversen. Do you read?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The relay on the pass answers like gravel shaken in a tin. He keys again. He will keep keying.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="37">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA at the radio, coat on indoors, Teo&apos;s log beside her and a pencil worn to a stub.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>...quad found... sixth bend... no rider...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(static)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>...walking. Toward the lake.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The hiss takes him. She turns to a clean page and says it out loud, slowly, because somebody in this hut ought to.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Left at nine. Down by one. He&apos;d look at everything twice, so call it noon before he left. The bike died near dark. Then he walked all night, because he&apos;s Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She adds it up and underlines the answer, the way Delacroix underlined his 23.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s at the lake.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Or he&apos;s somewhere between the sixth bend and the lake, and that&apos;s a bigger number.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She does not write that one down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(into the handset)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Iversen. Where&apos;s Delacroix?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>...fetching...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The hiss. She taps the pencil three times on the page, as if somebody might answer that.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="38">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX sits on his pack at the washout, bivvy bag strapped. He has been here all night, and it shows only in the way he stands up when the truck comes round the bend below. SOLLI gets out.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;re the inspector.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;m an inspector. Is the relief behind you?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The relief is at base eating soup. The pass is shut. Solli, park service. They said you&apos;d be counting things.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He considers being offended and decides it would take too long.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Did you find the boy?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I found his bike.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>In the cab, the road falls away under his window. His lips move.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Go on. Out loud. I&apos;d rather know.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-seven.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(next bend)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-six. The map says forty.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The map&apos;s right. There are two above the washout.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I walked those. I was being told not to count.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He pushed the bike off the road. In case someone needed to get past, I suppose.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Three weeks on his own. Nine nights calling a man who didn&apos;t answer.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And then he went to look.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Somebody had to.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You didn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She keeps her eyes on the road, which is the polite thing to do with a road like this.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>No.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-nine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-eight. You missed one.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Seeing if you&apos;d notice.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He counts. She lets him, all the way down.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="39">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para note="Second unit: the truck, wide, dust, no dialogue.">
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text></text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>From high on the ridge, the fire road is a pale thread laid down the mountain in loops. The truck is a green fleck on it, dragging a tail of dust that hangs in the still air.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>At the sixth bend, the quad in the ditch. The orange tape on its handlebar lifts once in the truck&apos;s wake, and falls.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Far below, the lake: a grey coin, one thread of smoke going straight up from its far shore.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="40">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - LAB - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A soldering iron heats on a gas ring, because the generator cannot be trusted with it today. The stripped spare transmitter lies open on the bench: a chassis and an opinion.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA saws its valve socket out, a square of old metal round it, and seats it in the punched lid of a tin that once held throat pastilles. Four flying leads. The valve fitted. It stands up out of the tin with wires hanging off it like the legs of something.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(to it)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Don&apos;t look at me like that.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the bench supply, the valve warms to a dull orange. The needle lifts. She shakes the tin hard, the way a quad shakes on a fire road. The needle shivers. Stays.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It holds, on a bench at the top of the mountain, where it is no use to anybody. She looks at the clock, at the door, at the four hours of track between here and the set that needs it. She wraps the tin in a sock.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="41">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA packs on the step: the tin in its sock, inside a mug, inside a second sock. Tools. A five-litre can of fuel, which she lifts twice to be sure it is as heavy as the first time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She writes on her knee:</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA. LEFT 11.40. GONE TO THE LOWER STATION WITH A PART. RADIO ON, LISTENING ON OURS. IF THE GENERATOR STOPS, KICK THE HOUSING LOW ON THE LEFT. BACK BY SIX.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She crosses out BACK BY SIX. Writes it again underneath.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She wedges the note in the door frame and pins it with a flat stone from the yard. Inside, she turns the radio up, so anyone who comes will hear it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Under its tarp, the generator coughs, and runs on. She goes down the track without looking back, because looking back at a generator has never once helped.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="42">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. LOWER STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Hours later. MARA comes down the track with the fuel can in the other hand from the one she started with.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The door is still propped with a stone. She reads the ground first: quad tyres coming in, going out deeper on a full tank; one set of boots on the step, back and forth, a man walking about while he thinks. Beside the step, an empty jerrycan with its cap screwed back on. Teo screws caps back on.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the doorstep, a clean pale square in the grit, where something flat lay for a long time. She puts her fingers on it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You took it with you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She leaves the door stone where it is. It is not hers to move.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="43">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. LOWER STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The cot made. The cold mug with its skin of tea, and beside it now Teo&apos;s, rinsed, upside down. The notebook: six o&apos;clock, six o&apos;clock, and on the last page an arrow and two initials.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA closes it the way you close a door on someone sleeping, and goes to work.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Out back, half her can into the cold generator, no more. Third pull, it catches. Inside, the dial brightens.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The back off the set, torch in her teeth. The output valve leans in its socket like a drunk against a wall. The socket is cracked through at one side. She keys.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Testing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The needle jumps for one word and drops.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One word and it cuts. There you are.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Out with the valve and the cracked socket. The pastille tin does not fit the gap. She makes it fit: wire, tape, a bent nail through the old screw hole. It looks like something a child made.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Leads soldered, valve in. She keys and holds the key down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One. Two. Three. Four. Five.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The needle stays up. A carrier, steady, for the first time in nine days, held in a pastille tin. She lets herself notice that, once.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base, this is the lower station. Do you read?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A breath of static. Then base answers, clean as a bell, as if someone were standing just behind her shoulder.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="44">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The big set speaks, clearly.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base, lower station. Do you read?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A mug stops halfway to somebody&apos;s mouth. IVERSEN is at the set before the DISPATCHER has her headset on.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Lower station, I read you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Mara. The set here&apos;s been one word at a time for nine days. It&apos;s as many as I like now. Ansel walked out toward the lake. Teo went after him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Solli has Delacroix. They&apos;re going down. There&apos;s a plane for first light.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;re not coming through the relay.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s low enough to see you from there. He never needed it. It was only ever the valve.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The room lets its breath out. Somebody laughs, short, and stops.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen does not smile. He takes the dispatcher&apos;s pencil and writes the time in the log, small and neat: 14.52. Underlines it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Stay on the air, lower station. Six o&apos;clock.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Six o&apos;clock.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="45">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BOATHOUSE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Afternoon light up through the floor. ANSEL sleeps, or near it. TEO darns a jumper with net twine, badly. AUD guts a fish without looking at it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The receiver hisses, as it has for nine days. Then a voice comes out of it, small and clean, over the hiss instead of under it: Mara, calling base. Lower station, do you read.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo stands up so fast the crate goes over.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s Mara. She fixes things.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>She&apos;s fixed his.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo snatches up the receiver.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Mara. It&apos;s Teo. We&apos;re at the lake, he&apos;s here, he&apos;s...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He stops. It has on and it has off.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>And then he laughs: helpless, out loud, the first time in three weeks. It hurts a little and he does not stop. Aud looks at him as if he has done something she has waited a long time to see someone do in this room.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo sets the receiver by Ansel&apos;s hand. Ansel finds his wrist and presses it. Once. Twice. Three times.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I know. I heard her too.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="46">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. KETTLE LAKE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The shingle before the boathouse. The sky high and white and empty, which is what they want from it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>AUD drags a net off the line. TEO takes the other end without being asked.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Straight. Pull it tight or it reads as a smudge.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A second net across the first: a cross, twenty paces each way, dark on pale stones. Then the white skiff, keel-up, set down at the crossing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;ve done this before.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Eleven winters ago. My husband went out on the ice. Same nets. Same boat.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Did they come?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They came.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He waits for the rest. She lets him wait.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s what the cross is for. It isn&apos;t for what happens after.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>This Delacroix. Is he coming?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He said he would.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>People say a lot of things.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s not people. He&apos;s Delacroix.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then we&apos;d better have something to feed him.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="47">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The truck going down. DELACROIX watches the rocks at the road&apos;s edge. SOLLI watches the road.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Below the sixth bend, he puts a hand on the dashboard. She stops. He walks back and crouches by a rock. On its uphill face, where a walker would see it and a truck going down would not: a chalk arrow, pointing down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I drove past that this morning.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It wasn&apos;t for you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(rubbing the chalk)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>A week old. He didn&apos;t draw it. He followed it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They drive on, slower. An arrow at every bend. Two. Three. Four. Seven. At the eighth it is not on the rock but on a tree, low down, at the height of a man on his knees.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Eight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He was crawling by then.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He was still drawing arrows.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Nine. Ten. Eleven. At the next bend the arrow points off the road, across the ditch, straight down through the trees.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The road goes round by the dam. Another hour.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He didn&apos;t go round. Twelve.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The road does not go to the lake. The arrows do.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="48">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The truck on the verge at the twelfth arrow, the light going amber. SOLLI takes a first aid pack, a handheld radio and a flare gun in a plastic case.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;ve got about half an hour of light.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-five minutes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She locks the truck. Then, after a look at the forest, unlocks it and leaves the keys under the seat.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>In case someone needs to get past.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They go down through the trees, trunk to trunk, Solli easily, Delacroix carefully, which is not the same thing and takes longer. Pale scuffs of chalk at knee height, enough to say: still this way. He touches each one. His lips move.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The trees stop. The lake below in the last of the light, the boathouse across it, and on the shingle a dark cross with something white at its centre.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Somebody&apos;s expecting a plane.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Somebody&apos;s expecting someone.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="49">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. KETTLE LAKE - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX and SOLLI come out onto the far shore. Across the bay a headlamp comes on, one small steady point.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It blinks. Off, on. Off, on.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How do you know?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s counting.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Solli answers with her torch. Across the water, a figure goes to the white skiff and with one heave rights it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>There goes the signal.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The skiff comes across, AUD at the oars, rowing the way some people walk. She beaches it with one long stroke. TEO is out before it stops, wet to the knees again, with nothing rehearsed at all.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You came.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>We said we would.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo shakes his hand, and forgets to let go of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Is he alive?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s alive.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Boat takes three. One of them&apos;s me.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Take him first. He&apos;s older.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;m not older than you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You are, though.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix gets into the bow the way a man gets into cold water: all at once, because slowly would be worse. Aud pushes off.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(barely audible)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nine. Ten. Eleven.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>If you count my strokes, I&apos;ll take more of them.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twelve.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She takes the next stroke longer, deliberately. He counts it anyway.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="50">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BOATHOUSE - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Five people in one room over the water. Stove going hard, steam on the window, nowhere to stand that is not in someone&apos;s way.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>SOLLI kneels by the cot, unwinding the splint one knot at a time. ANSEL watches her face, because that is where the news is.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Who set this?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I did. It&apos;s an oar.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It&apos;s a good oar.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(rewrapping it in Aud&apos;s knots)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He goes out by air. He won&apos;t stand the fire road.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She takes the handheld out onto the jetty.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (O.S.) (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Dispatch, Solli. Kettle Lake boathouse. Both found. One walking, one not...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX sits by the cot. He and Ansel have met before; it is in both faces.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I went up to the cairn. The first night.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-five.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Something happens at the corner of Ansel&apos;s mouth, a long way from a smile but facing that way.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix holds out a hand to TEO without looking. Teo gives him the stone, warm from his pocket. Delacroix puts it in Ansel&apos;s hand and closes the fingers round it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Ansel lifts his other hand. One finger raised. One stone short.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>What does it mean?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>A cairn knocked down tells you there was weather. Rebuilt, it tells you there was a keeper. Rebuilt one stone short, it tells you the keeper wants somebody to come looking for the stone.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He knew someone would count it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He knew I would.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Ansel&apos;s eyes close, not from fever this time. From having been understood.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>AUD at the stove does not pretend she was not listening.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He can be understood after soup.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>SOLLI comes back in with the cold on her.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Plane&apos;s on for first light. If the weather lets it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It always lets it, until it doesn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the shelf, the receiver hisses. Aud looks at the clock and turns it up.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Six.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The room goes quiet without being asked.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="51">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. LOWER STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The generator rough but running. The pastille tin glowing faintly through its punched holes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA sits where Ansel sat. At the top of the mountain, a note on a door says she is back by now.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Lower station calling the line. Lower station calling the line.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She does not say goodnight first.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Faint, through the relay, the second station answers: a man saying his station&apos;s name and nothing else. Then the third: a woman, brisk, a kettle on behind her. Then the silence where the top station should be.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Cold Spring. Keeper away. Mara, answering for it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Lower station. Keeper away. Mara, answering for it. On the air.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base. All stations heard. Solli called in from the boathouse. Both found. One walking, one not. Delacroix is with them. They&apos;re listening.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Mara puts the pencil down, parallel to the edge of the notebook, the way Teo would.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Goodnight, all stations. Goodnight, the boathouse. Goodnight, Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Goodnight, Mara.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The hiss comes back, softer, like something settling. She does not count anything. She lets the room do it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <method_storyteller kind="section" level="1"/>
      <text>ACT THREE</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <method_storyteller kind="synopsis"/>
      <text>The plane, the storm, and forty-four rungs.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="52">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - DAWN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The base before the day staff. One lamp, the kettle on. IVERSEN at the big set with his coat on. He has not been home. The coat knows it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The DISPATCHER has the log open and two pencils: one for good news, one for the other kind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Search One, base. Radio check.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base, Search One. Loud and clear and a bit early. Off the water at six-oh-four, if anybody&apos;s keeping score.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen is. He writes 0604 on the pad.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Kettle Lake, the boathouse on the north bay. One casualty. Look for a cross of nets on the shingle.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Lovely spot. I&apos;ll bring a sandwich.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen rubs out the time beside PASS on the weather board with his thumb and chalks a new one. Earlier.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Search One, the front is running early. The pass closes by mid-afternoon.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Early I can work with. It&apos;s late that gets you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s cheerful.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They pick them for it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Write down the time he said that.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It goes in the log, in the pencil for the other kind.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="53">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. KETTLE LAKE - DAWN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Mist lies on the water so flat the lake looks paved. On the shingle, yesterday&apos;s cross of nets and the upturned skiff: white, the only clean edge in the world.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>TEO at the end of the jetty, hands in his armpits. AUD, the boathouse keeper, with a mug she is not drinking from. SOLLI, the ranger, crouched on the shingle, loading the truck&apos;s flare gun the way you load something you would rather not use twice.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How will he find it? In this.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He won&apos;t. He&apos;ll find above it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A drone, thin, coming and going as if the mist were tuning it. It moves left. Then right, which is worse.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then we&apos;d better tell him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She fires straight up. The flare climbs out of the mist into clear air nobody down here knew was there, and hangs, red, rocking on its little parachute.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s seen it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He&apos;s seen something.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The PLANE comes down through the top of the mist: small, yellow, two floats, low enough that every board of the jetty hums.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Solli looks in the pouch.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Two.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One night in a boathouse with Delacroix, and she has started doing it too.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="54">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. KETTLE LAKE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The mist has burned off to rags. The PLANE taxis in to the jetty with a care that looks like good manners.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The PILOT, 40s, steps off the float in a jacket with too many pockets.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Morning. Which of you&apos;s the casualty? Because you all look terrible.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Inside. Leg below the knee, splinted. Fever since...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX, on the boathouse step, coat buttoned to the throat.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ten days, today.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The pilot takes a notebook from one pocket, a pencil from another, a calculator from a third.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It&apos;s only arithmetic, so nobody take it personally. I go light, or I go late, and I don&apos;t go late. Stretcher, him, and one more under eighty kilos. With boots.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;ve got a truck on the fire road.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then you&apos;re not going. Good. That&apos;s one.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Nobody looks at Teo, who is standing very straight, as if that would make him heavier.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I can walk back up. It&apos;s my station.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody&apos;s asking you to walk. I&apos;m asking what you weigh.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Sixty-one.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Sixty-four.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Three weeks ago.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo opens his mouth and finds nothing in it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty minutes. Then I go, with whoever&apos;s on the float.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He sits on the jetty and eats a sandwich from a fourth pocket, watching the weather like a film he has already seen.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="55">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BOATHOUSE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Light off the water moves on the ceiling. ANSEL on the cot, splinted with an oar handle, eyes open, the cairn stone on his chest under one hand, the way you hold a ticket.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>AUD packs him a bag without consulting anyone. DELACROIX by the door. TEO by the cot, where he has been all night.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You go with him. You&apos;ve listened to him not talk for nine days. Nobody else is qualified.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You don&apos;t leave a station.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You left it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>That lands. Teo takes it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then let me go back now.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Your station has a keeper.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Who?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>She fixes things.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>She&apos;ll hate it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>She&apos;ll do it properly and hate it. With her it&apos;s the same thing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Ansel&apos;s free hand finds Teo&apos;s sleeve, and tugs. Once. Twice. Three times.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(not looking up)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s yes. He does three for yes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>What does he do for no?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He hasn&apos;t needed it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo sits down on the edge of the cot. He has lost, and it does not look like losing.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="56">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. BOATHOUSE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The boathouse door is off its hinges. Aud took it down with a screwdriver and no help, and nobody argued. ANSEL lies on it, tied down with net cord, the stone still under his hand.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>SOLLI takes the head end, TEO the foot. The PILOT walks alongside, holding nothing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Mind the third board. The third board&apos;s a liar.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;ve been here before.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;ve been on a lot of jetties. They all have a third board.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo&apos;s arms shake. He does not say so. At the float, with quiet swearing from the pilot, Ansel goes in sideways, door and all.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Bring it back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;ll bring it back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>AUD holds out a tin to Teo, its paint worn to the metal where thumbs have opened it for years.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Open it on the flight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She keeps hold of it a second longer than she needs to, and gives him the look she gave him over the oar, with the suspicion taken out and nothing put in its place.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Don&apos;t drop it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I won&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I didn&apos;t mean the tin.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She walks back up the jetty and steps over the third board without looking down.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="57">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. KETTLE LAKE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The PLANE taxis out to the middle of the lake, where there is room to be wrong. Through the side window: TEO, in a seat facing backwards, the tin on his knees, one hand on the blankets over Ansel.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(over the intercom)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>All right back there?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Yes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s a no. Hold on to him, not the seat.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Two white wakes open behind the floats like a zip. The floats lift, touch, lift and stay lifted.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the jetty, AUD, arms folded. She does not wave. Then she does, once, the hand barely above the shoulder.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>In the window, Teo has the tin open. Biscuits, in rows. On top, a square of paper, in pencil: 6 o&apos;clock.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>She waved.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They usually do. Eventually.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="58">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Day staff in the background: one on the phone to the hospital, one making tea nobody will drink. IVERSEN at the set, his coat on the chair behind him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base, Search One. Off the water at ten-fifty. Two aboard, one stretcher. And a door. I&apos;ve promised to bring the door back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(writing)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Two aboard, one stretcher. And a door.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen moves his coat to the back of the chair, and sits down. The room notices. Nobody says so.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You haven&apos;t sat down in four days.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;ve been busy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;ve been standing. It isn&apos;t the same.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Read me the time again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ten-fifty.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It is written. He wanted to hear it said.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="59">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. LOWER STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Ansel&apos;s station, kept Ansel&apos;s way: cot made, mug upside down on the drainer. MARA at the set. Behind the grille the valve glows, steady, seated, hers.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Through the relay hiss, in pieces:</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>...two aboard, one stretcher. Lower Station, copy?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Copy. Who&apos;s the second?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The boy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Good.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The front&apos;s early. It shuts the pass tonight and sits, three days, maybe four. If you&apos;re going back up to the top station, go now. Otherwise you stay put.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How long on foot?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Six hours. If the ridge is kind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Is it ever?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It was once. I was younger. Lower Station, I&apos;m losing...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Hiss. She hangs the headphones where Ansel hangs them, the cord coiled three times.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody up there, then.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She says it the way you say the name of a dog that has run off.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="60">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. LOWER STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>High above, the wind works along the ridge like someone looking for a way in.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA comes out with her pack on. Teo&apos;s jerrycan stands by the lean-to. She tips it with her boot: a third left. He stopped short of the last of it, the way you leave the last biscuit. She straps it to the pack, which complains.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She shuts the station door. Opens it again. No stone on the step: Ansel&apos;s went down the valley with Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She picks one from the yard, flat and grey, the kind Delacroix would approve of without saying so, and props the door with it, open a hand&apos;s width.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>No chalk. No arrow. The stone says the other thing a keeper&apos;s stone can say: someone has been here.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She goes up the track and does not look back.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="61">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The truck comes up the switchbacks faster than the gravel likes, under a sky the colour of a bruise on its second day.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>In the cab: SOLLI driving, DELACROIX with the map on his knee, unread.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How many?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-two. To the washout. I count down on the way up. It&apos;s quicker.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It isn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It feels quicker.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Your engineer. Will she have gone back up?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>She doesn&apos;t like being told to stay put.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody does.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody does it as much as she doesn&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The rain arrives the way it does up here: all at once, and from the side.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Three. Two.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I can see it, Delacroix.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The washout. She stops where the road stops.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Two hours to the top. Less, if we don&apos;t talk.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then don&apos;t.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Solli pockets the flare gun, two left, and takes the rope. They go round the washout on the rock, Delacroix first because he has done it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The truck sits where it was left, wipers stopped halfway, like somebody interrupted mid-sentence.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="62">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The storm comes up the valley like a wall pushed by somebody who means it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA comes up the last of the track bent under the pack and the can. Six hours. The ridge was not kind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She stops at the cairn because her legs decide for her. She has never counted anything she did not have to. She counts now, touching each stone.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-three. Twenty-four.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A gust hits her. She holds on to the cairn, and it holds.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Twenty-five.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She has no idea whether that is the right number. She has a feeling Delacroix would. Any stone at her feet would do. It is not her cairn, and it is not her count.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Above her the mast has started to sing, and it is not one note, and it is not two. The wind is from the east. They don&apos;t get an east wind. They got one once.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="63">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The hut has gone cold in the way of a room nobody expected back. The tarp on the north wall strains at its lashings. Outside, the generator coughs on and off, like an argument.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA takes the can straight back out. Through the window, her torch at the generator. The coughing stops. Starts. Steadies, nearly.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She comes back in wet to the shoulder and sits at the radio.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Field Station to Base.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Hiss. The relay, or the antenna, or the weather, or all three, which is how it usually goes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She holds the handset, thumb off the key, and talks to it anyway, as if Teo were on the other end and asleep.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;d have asked me why I took the job. He did. People think it&apos;s the quiet. My father fixed things in a town, for people who stood behind him and told him it had been fine yesterday. He&apos;d find the bolt with no thread, and they&apos;d say it had always been like that, and it had. Nobody down there tells you what&apos;s broken. They&apos;d rather you didn&apos;t look.

Up here nothing pretends. The wind shows you the gap. The generator tells you it&apos;s dying, out loud, every night. You told the radio every night that the fourth station didn&apos;t answer, and nobody wrote it down. I&apos;m writing it down. That&apos;s why. Somebody has to say it&apos;s broken, and fix it, and not need thanking. I don&apos;t need thanking. I&apos;d just like somebody, once, to have noticed.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Her thumb has not been on the key the whole time. She keys it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Field Station. Still here.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The hiss comes back, ordinary, and she finds that will do.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="64">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para note="Rain towers and wind: scenes 64, 65 and 67. Scene 66 is the base, and dry.">
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text></text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The storm is unpacking. The guy wires shriek in four keys. The fuel drum walks across the yard on its own.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX comes up the last of the slope with SOLLI behind him on a rope. They are ten yards off when the hut door opens. MARA stands in it, the light behind her.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You came.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You had the door open before we knocked.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Teo does that.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He said it&apos;s different, seeing it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It is.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Solli. Park service. I&apos;d shake your hand, but I can&apos;t find mine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Mara. Come in, it&apos;s warmer.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>All three of them know the rest of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It isn&apos;t. But it&apos;s out of the wind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix stops on the step and looks at the generator. Running. Not well. Running.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You fuelled it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>A third of a can. Teo left it. It&apos;ll go till morning.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And then?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then it&apos;s morning.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The wind slams the door behind them, the way it did behind Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="65">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Three people round a table built for one. SOLLI has the chair, because she is the guest. DELACROIX has an upturned crate, which is beneath him, which he knows.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The tarp on the north wall goes for the third time since Teo left, all three lashings at once, and the storm is in the room. The stove flame lies down. The tins walk.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Board.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Bunkhouse.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She is out of the door, Solli after her. Delacroix puts his back to the gap. It is about the size of him, which is the first time that has been useful.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They come back with a bunk board and put it over the gap. It fits, nearly, which up here is the same as fits.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nails.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix finds them in her coat pocket and puts them in her hand one at a time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Six. Seven. Eight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Eight&apos;s enough.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She drives them with the back of a spanner. The storm leans on the board and the board leans back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You took the one with no names on it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody&apos;s name should face the weather.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The room is a room again, smaller than before. Delacroix spoons beans into three mugs, the same number of spoons each.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Did he go easily? The boy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He went sideways. With a door.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Is it always like this up here?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>No.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Usually there&apos;s only one of us.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="66">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Rain on the window. Background: one of the day staff asleep across two chairs.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>IVERSEN at the set. The relay light, which has flickered for four days, goes steady. Then out. Switch, wait, switch. Nothing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s it. For good. Log it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The DISPATCHER writes the time, then turns back through the pages and reads, in the voice people keep for names.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>&quot;Eighteen hundred. Top station calling the line. Fourth station, no answer.&quot; Nine nights of that. &quot;Eighteen hundred. Lower station calling. All stations answered.&quot; &quot;Ten-fifty. Two aboard, one stretcher. And a door.&quot; &quot;Nineteen-twelve. Relay failed.&quot;</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Write the top station. Unknown.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That isn&apos;t a log entry.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It is tonight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>It goes in. The pencil, Iversen notices, is the one for good news.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="67">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RADIO MAST - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The antenna has been torn off true and swings on one bracket, banging the steel like somebody asking to be let in.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA in a harness, a torch taped to her shoulder. DELACROIX braced against the fuel drum, the safety line through his gloves. She clips on and climbs.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One. Two. Three.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She climbs. He counts. The wind no longer pulls at her like it wants her for something. It has decided.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Eleven. Twelve.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A gust leans the mast. She stops, cheek to the steel. He stops counting.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Don&apos;t stop.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirteen.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The line goes taut in his hands and stays taut.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-one. Thirty-two.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She gets an arm round the antenna and holds it true with her whole body.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Forty-four.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I know. I&apos;m here.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One turn. The wind takes it out of true. She drags it back. Two turns. Three. The mast&apos;s shriek drops to something that sounds as if it has noticed.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Is that it?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He counts her down, all forty-four, backwards, and she lets him say every one.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="68">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - LATER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA at the set, still in the harness, dripping. DELACROIX behind her. SOLLI on the cot in a blanket.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base, Field Station.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A click, and a voice, patient, a long way off, but clean.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Field Station, Iversen. I hear you. There&apos;s no relay. You&apos;re coming straight over the ridge.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(leaning in)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Three here. Mara, the ranger, me.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Three. I&apos;m writing it down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They can hear the pencil.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Tell him the truck&apos;s at the washout. Nobody tows it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I heard. Nobody tows it. Field Station, they landed. He&apos;s with the doctors. The boy is sitting outside on a chair with a tin on his knee and won&apos;t give it to anybody.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Mara lets go of the key. A moment.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Copy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (V.O.)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Six o&apos;clock tomorrow. You call the line.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix puts his hand on her wet shoulder, briefly. Neither of them mentions it.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="69">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - BUNKHOUSE - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Four bunks, three boards. SOLLI on a lower bunk in all her clothes, her torch on the names carved by years of relief teams. ANSEL, three times, years apart. The photograph: four keepers at a summer handover, all smiling, none of them now.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She takes out a penknife. Opens it. Closes it. She has not earned a place on that wall.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Torch off. The mast has found its one long note again, as if the east wind had never happened.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Goodnight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>To the photograph, or the names, or the mast. She lies awake listening to it.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="70">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A lull. Over the station the storm has stopped to draw breath.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX in the yard, no torch. The fuel drum has walked a yard toward the edge. He puts his shoulder to it and walks it back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The generator runs. Even. Not a cough in it. He listens the way you listen to somebody breathing in the next room.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>His hand goes to his watch, out of a habit older than the station, and stops.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>No.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He pulls his sleeve down over it and goes back in.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="71">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The board over the gap creaks like a ship. MARA on the cot, boots off for the first time in four days. DELACROIX in the chair, the handset on the table, not in his fist.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I saw a light. The first night, from the mast. Low on the far slope, where the map has nothing. Then it went out.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I know. You didn&apos;t come down for a minute and a half. You&apos;re never a minute and a half.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>If I&apos;d said, we&apos;d have gone that night.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>In the dark, down a slope we didn&apos;t know, with one torch.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He was signalling.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Aud says he came in with a lamp and no oil in it. He used it all up on us.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And I didn&apos;t say.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Somebody saw it. Up here, that counts.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You should sleep.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You should stop saying that.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She almost laughs, and turns to the wall. He turns the radio down. Not off.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <method_storyteller kind="section" level="1"/>
      <text>ACT FOUR</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <method_storyteller kind="synopsis"/>
      <text>Everyone answers.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="72">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - DAWN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The storm has gone in the night the way it came, all at once, without explaining itself. Everything glitters: frost on the guy wires, ice on the generator tarp.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA comes out with a mug in each hand and looks at the sky, which is blue from one ridge to the other. She does not trust it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX is at the edge of the yard, looking down the valley. SOLLI comes out of the bunkhouse with her boots in one hand.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Is it always like this after? Like it&apos;s sorry.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It isn&apos;t sorry. It&apos;s resting.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Mara holds out the second mug. Delacroix turns for it. Solli takes it. Delacroix turns back to the valley as if that was what he meant to do.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Through the open door the radio wakes. Voices: a man out of breath, a woman laughing at him, somebody reading out a position and being corrected.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They&apos;re through the pass.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Four.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;ll get the truck down before they want the road.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thank you. For the switchbacks.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;d have counted them anyway.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Yes. But you let me.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She hands Mara the empty mug and starts down toward the washout. She does not look back. People who drive for a living rarely do.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="73">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Daylight through the long window, for the first time in four days. Somebody has wiped CLOSED off the weather board and written OPEN.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>IVERSEN sits at the big set with his coat still on. Base staff (bg) move slowly, the way people move after something heavy has been put down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The telephone on the wall rings. Nobody uses the telephone. The DISPATCHER answers it, listens, writes, and covers the mouthpiece.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The hospital. He keeps the leg. They say he complained the whole time, in a whisper.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>He would.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He holds out his hand for the phone. He holds it to his ear and opens his mouth, and the hospital waits, politely, a long way off.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He hands it back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Tell them thank you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She does, and hangs up, and writes in the log.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>What did you write?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ten forty. Hospital. Ansel keeps the leg.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Underline it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She underlines it. Then, because he is still watching, she underlines it again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen takes his coat off.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="74">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para note="Second unit: the truck going down, wide.">
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text></text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>From high above, the ridge road: grey switchbacks laid down the mountain like a dropped rope. One small truck on it, going down. No dust today. The storm has laid all of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>At the washout an arm comes out of the driver&apos;s window, one hand raised, to nobody. Up on the ridge, nobody is looking.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>IN THE CAB</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>SOLLI drives with one wrist over the wheel and picks up the handset.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Dispatch, Solli. Road&apos;s open to the washout. After that it&apos;s legs.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She takes the next bend, and the next. Her lips move. She catches herself at nine and stops, annoyed.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s his job.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She takes the tenth without counting it, which costs her something.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="75">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The quad lies where Teo pushed it, nose down in the heather, a cobweb strung from the handlebar to the mirror.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>SOLLI crouches at the fuel line, at the leak she found three days ago, and wraps tape round it, which will make no difference to anything.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>RANGER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You did fine. You got him most of the way.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She chains it to the truck and pulls away. The quad follows, rattling over every stone like a drawer of cutlery going down a staircase.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>At the bend, a chalk arrow on a rock, half washed off by the storm. Still pointing. Solli slows beside it for as long as it takes the quad to stop rattling. Then she drives on.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="76">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. KETTLE LAKE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The lake is flat and pale and very still, as if it has been told to behave.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the shingle the nets still lie in their cross around the upturned white skiff. AUD takes it apart the way she laid it: one net at a time, shaken out, folded, laid over her arm.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Behind her, through the open boathouse door, the old receiver is on. It is not six o&apos;clock. Base is calling the line in daylight, which it never does: a count after the storm.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She stops folding.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One by one the stations answer. She knows every voice. The second. The third. A pause. Then the lower station, a voice she does not know, careful, reading off a card.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Took your time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Then the top station: Mara, one word, and done.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Aud has listened to all of it. She always does. She turns the skiff right way up and drags it to the water. The shingle is only shingle.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="77">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BOATHOUSE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The cot where Ansel lay is stripped, the blanket folded square at its foot. An oar by the door is missing its handle.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The PILOT comes in without waiting to be asked, a crate on one shoulder and a box under his arm.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Groceries. Paraffin. Tea, the good kind. I was told to say the good kind.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He hands AUD a folded list. She reads it. It is in order of size.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How is he?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Tidy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He opens the box: a radio, newer than anything in the room.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base sent it. It talks back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I don&apos;t want it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They said you&apos;d say that.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He wires it up anyway, the aerial out of the window to the gutter, while she watches and does not help.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;ve listened to a radio for thirty years.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>This one listens back. It&apos;s the park&apos;s. Yours to look after.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She thinks about that, as if it might be a trick. Then she puts the new radio on the shelf and moves the old receiver one inch to the left to make room. It is the most she will concede, and they both know it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She takes down two cups, puts one back, and takes it down again.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="78">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. BOATHOUSE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Afternoon. The PILOT refuels the plane on the jetty.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>AUD sits on the step with the new handset in her lap, the lead running through the window like a leash. She checks her watch. A little after three. It is not six, and it is not tomorrow.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She keys it anyway.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>This is the lake. The boathouse. On the lake.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A voice comes back at once, clear as water from a tap: base, pleased, asking her to say again...</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She switches it off.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You start tomorrow. Six o&apos;clock.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I was practising.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How did it go?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>BOATHOUSE KEEPER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>They answer very fast.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She does not put the handset down. She sits with it a while longer, in case.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="79">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. KETTLE LAKE - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Out by the reed point, where Ansel came down to the water, a SECOND BOAT CREW (bg) lower an orange marker buoy from a rowboat, then sit and look at it, because there is nothing else to do with a buoy.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The plane taxis out from the jetty.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>IN THE COCKPIT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>PILOT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base, Kettle Lake, outbound. One aboard. Me and an empty seat.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(listens; smiles)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And your boathouse is on the air. She hung up on you. I&apos;d take that as a yes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>BACK TO SCENE</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The plane lifts and banks low over the buoy, waggles its wings once, which is not regulation, and climbs away toward the pass.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The buoy goes on bobbing where somebody got as far as the water.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="80">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. LOWER STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The door stands propped with a flat stone, the way Mara left it. DELACROIX sits beside it on the step. He has counted the slates on the lean-to roof. Twenty, two cracked.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The RELIEF TEAM (bg) come down the track, four of them, heavy packs, talking. They stop talking when they see him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Among them, the NEW KEEPER (bg): a broad, quiet man with a beard he has clearly grown for the job. Delacroix shakes his hand, once, properly.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;re the lower station. The door stays as it is until you&apos;ve read the notebook. It means someone has been here. Someone who meant to come back.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(counts them with his eyes)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Two of you stay. Two come up with me.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He turns back to the new keeper.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The transmitter has a carrier made out of a tin. Don&apos;t touch the tin. Six o&apos;clock, every night. If you can&apos;t answer, light the lamp on the slope. Somebody up top will see it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Eventually.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The new keeper nods. Delacroix goes inside.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="81">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. LOWER STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The room as neat as the first night. The radio dial glows green, steady now. Inside the open panel, the tin carrier, with a strip of Mara&apos;s tape across it that says DON&apos;T.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX opens Ansel&apos;s notebook. Six o&apos;clock. Six o&apos;clock. The hand getting smaller down the page, as if the man writing it were going a long way off, which he was.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He counts the entries with one finger.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He takes out his pencil, worn to a stub. Below the last entry, small and neat, he writes the date, and one word.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>INSERT: THE NOTEBOOK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The date, and &quot;answered&quot;.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>BACK TO SCENE</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He does not underline it. It does not need it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He leaves the notebook open beside the radio and goes out to tell them they can close the door.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="82">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The cairn in clear daylight. Down the valley, smoke from the lower station&apos;s pipe, for the first time since Ansel left it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX comes up the spur onto the ridge. MARA is already at the cairn with a stone in both hands: flat, grey, a rusty ring on one face.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s from the yard.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It held the generator tarp down for three weeks. It&apos;s done its time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>The other one went with him. Solli says he wouldn&apos;t let go of it for the X-ray.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Then it&apos;s his.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She sets the new stone on top. It rocks. She turns it a quarter turn and chinks a sliver of rock under one edge, carefully, as if somebody is going to check.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Somebody is going to check.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix takes his gloves off and touches each stone with a bare finger, top to bottom, the way he did in the dark. His lips move.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He starts again at the top. Slower. Every stone.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He puts his gloves back on and says nothing. From Delacroix this is a speech, and Mara has worked with him long enough to hear all of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Good.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They stand a while, sighted down the valley like the cairn, toward the smoke.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="83">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Two of the RELIEF TEAM (bg) come up the last of the slope, the NEW TOP KEEPER (bg) between them: early twenties, a new coat, a pack too big for her and very well packed. She has the look of someone who has rehearsed what to say and forgotten all of it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA waits in the doorway until they arrive.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You came.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The new keeper opens her mouth. Nothing comes.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s all right. Nobody remembers it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>DELACROIX looks at her boots and nods. That is a welcome, and she seems to know it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>At the generator Mara lifts the tarp. Six new bolts. She taps each one.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Every one has a thread. If one ever turns in your fingers and keeps turning, it&apos;s lying to you. Take it out.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The stone that held the tarp is gone. The new keeper looks round the yard for another, as if she is meant to do it now. Delacroix approves of this privately.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The mast takes the wind and gives back one long low note. The new keeper looks up.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It sings in a north wind. One note. In a west wind it&apos;s two, and they don&apos;t agree.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And an east wind?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>We got one once.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She goes in first, the way the keeper does.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="84">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The map is back above the radio, creased across the middle from the night it came down. Four pins.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA touches the lowest pin. It is only a pin. She moves nothing.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Beside the map, DELACROIX looks at what he wrote the first night: 23, underlined. He licks his thumb and rubs. It smears into a grey cloud with a line under it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You could leave it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It was wrong.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It wasn&apos;t wrong. It just wasn&apos;t enough.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He takes a rubber from his pocket and works at it properly. The underline goes last.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>How many was it? In the end.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I stopped counting.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She looks at him.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Ninety-one. I stopped writing them down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>What is left is a pale patch, cleaner than the wall around it. You would only see it if you knew.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="85">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. FIELD STATION - LAB - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The bench, cleared, the spare parts sorted into tins. MARA squares Teo&apos;s logbooks with the flat of her hand.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She opens the top one at the last page. Twenty-one days of perfect entries. Nine of &quot;Called the line. 3 answered.&quot; Then her own hand, in his format.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She writes the date and, in his format:</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>INSERT: THE LOGBOOK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>&quot;Called the line. 4 answered.&quot;</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>BACK TO SCENE</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Her pencil hovers. There is room on the line for something more. She does not write it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>There.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She hears herself say it to an empty room, and does not take it back. She puts the pencil in the spine, where Teo kept it, and turns off the lamp.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="86">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The door lets in a slice of cold and TEO, on his own two feet, in a hospital jumper a size too large, hair combed flat with water. He looks as if he has been folded and put away carefully and then taken out again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The DISPATCHER takes off her headset.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He has never heard his name in this room except through a radio.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>IVERSEN stands. He is older than his voice. Teo shakes his hand, then the dispatcher&apos;s, then Iversen&apos;s again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Sorry. It&apos;s different. Seeing it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It is.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He turns the log round so Teo can read it: nine nights, each logged late, each with Teo&apos;s name.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nine nights. Nobody passed it on. I&apos;m sorry. I should have answered you.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo has rehearsed nothing for this.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You&apos;re answering now.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Iversen almost smiles. He turns his own chair at the big set toward Teo.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Sit there. It&apos;s warmer.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Parenthetical"/>
      <text>(beat)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It isn&apos;t. But it&apos;s out of the draught.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Teo sits.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="87">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. RIDGE ROAD - DAY</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA and DELACROIX come down from the hut, packs on, the wind behind them for once.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>At the first bend below the yard, under the grass, Delacroix stops.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Forty.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You started at thirty-eight last time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I was two out. The grass has two. You can&apos;t see them coming up.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The next, only a shelf of rock under the turf.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-nine.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The washout. They go round on the rock, Mara first. The gravel holds this time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-eight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Thirty-eight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>SERIES OF SHOTS</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>A) Thirty, twenty-nine. Two voices, out of step.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>B) Twenty. In step now, the cloud lifting off the ridge behind them like a lid somebody finally remembered.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>C) Nine. Mara says it a beat before he does.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>BACK TO SCENE</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The last bend, where the grass gives up and lets the gravel have it. They take it together.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>One.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He unfolds the paper map and looks at it for the first time in five days.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Forty. The map was right.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>You sound disappointed.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I&apos;d got used to it lying.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He folds it to the same square, puts it away, and follows her a pace behind, the way he walked up.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="88">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIRE ROAD - DUSK</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The fire road along the far shoulder of the hill, gravel pink in the last light. Far below, Kettle Lake: a grey coin going dark.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>MARA and DELACROIX walk it side by side. At the bend where the chalk arrow was, the rock is clean.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Down at the end of the boathouse jetty, a light comes on. One small steady point. It does not flicker.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>There&apos;s a light.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She says it at once, this time.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>I see it.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>MARA</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>It&apos;s steady.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DELACROIX</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>She means it to be.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Delacroix does not count anything. He keeps his hands in his pockets and looks at the light.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>They walk on, and the light stays on the water behind them, getting smaller, and does not go out.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="89">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>INT. BASE - RADIO ROOM - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>One minute to six. The DISPATCHER has the log open at a clean page. TEO sits at the big set with the handset in both hands. IVERSEN stands behind him, having decided not to help.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Base to the line. Six o&apos;clock.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The top station: a young woman, a little too fast. Tick. The second, an old voice, bored, the best sound in the world. Tick. The third. Tick. A gap. Then the lower station, careful, reading off a card. Tick.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO (CONT&apos;D)</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>That&apos;s four.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>He reaches for the key. Iversen&apos;s hand on his shoulder. Wait. Ten seconds of hiss.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Then a woman&apos;s voice, gruff, no call sign, says one word: &quot;Boathouse.&quot; The line clicks dead.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The room laughs. Teo laughs loudest.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>She answered. Write it down.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Who&apos;s calling?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Everybody.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>DISPATCHER</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>And who isn&apos;t?</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>IVERSEN</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Nobody.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>She writes it, and underlines it, and does not need to be asked.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Character"/>
      <text>TEO</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Dialogue"/>
      <text>Goodnight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Down the line, one by one, small and far off, they say it back. Even, after a while, the lake.</text>
    </para>
    <para scene_number="90">
      <style basestylename="Scene Heading"/>
      <text>EXT. FIELD STATION - NIGHT</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The yard at night. Clear, cold, stars down to the ridge on every side.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The generator runs under its tarp, even and ordinary, a new stone on the corner, a little small for the job. Above it the mast takes a north wind and gives back one long low note.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>In the hut window the radio light glows. Behind it the NEW TOP KEEPER (bg) writes in a logbook, carefully, in a hand that will get smaller down the page as the weeks go on and then, one day, bigger again.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>On the ridge the cairn stands sighted down the valley. Twenty-six stones. Nobody counting them tonight.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>Down the valley the lower station shows one lamp. Further off, at the edge of the lake, another. Everything that is lit is lit on purpose.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action"/>
      <text>The mast holds its note.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Transition"/>
      <text>FADE OUT.</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Action" align="center"/>
      <text>THE END</text>
    </para>
  </paragraphs>
  <titlepage>
    <para bookmark="Title">
      <style basestylename="Normal Text" align="center"/>
      <text>FIELD STATION</text>
    </para>
    <para>
      <style basestylename="Normal Text"/>
      <text></text>
    </para>
    <para bookmark="Credit">
      <style basestylename="Normal Text" align="center"/>
